WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:04.737
[Music starts]

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:09.440
[Nash Rochman, PhD] There's a very&nbsp;
old saying in physics that "it's great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.360
to be able to explain but it's better to predict".

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:20.160
But it's also true that it's impossible to predict&nbsp;
without an understanding, without an explanation.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.080
If we're ever going to have a deeper&nbsp;
understanding of of any biological system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.162
for example, a relationship between a novel&nbsp;
virus and a human host, we're going to need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.162 --> 00:00:33.297
to have a deeper understanding of host-pathogen&nbsp;
relationships with the information we have today.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.640
So I study host-pathogen interactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.840
and these could be human hosts and&nbsp;
viral pathogens like SARS-Cov2.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.000
Host-pathogen relationships involve cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.760
in the host, or viruses in the host,&nbsp;
or bacterial systems in the host.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.994
They could be animal hosts&nbsp;
and bacterial pathogens. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.994 --> 00:00:56.000
could even be bacterial hosts and viral pathogens.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:03.120
And I want to find unifying forces that shape&nbsp;
the interactions between hosts and pathogens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.800
to predict how the biology of the host can shape&nbsp;
the evolution of the pathogen, and vice versa.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.236
And this is important obviously for just&nbsp;
understanding basic problems in biology.

00:01:14.236 --> 00:01:16.800
But also, I think this is an&nbsp;
interesting area because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.360
provides immediate practical application as well.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.600
Obviously, if you can find some forces&nbsp;
that are shaping viral evolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.960
you might be able to use that information&nbsp;
to help design better vaccines.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.320
If you find forces that are&nbsp;
shaping bacterial evolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.840
in response to a virus, you might be able to&nbsp;
find ways to better invent new antibiotics.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.713
So, with more and more data,&nbsp;
you can start predicting when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.713 --> 00:01:43.520
the next pandemic will be or&nbsp;
what the next pandemic will be.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.800
If you can predict, before it happens, you&nbsp;
could even prevent the first human infection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.760
by mitigating the spread of that virus&nbsp;
within the intermediate host population.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.867
And if you're really good, you&nbsp;
can make that time negative.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.867 --> 00:02:00.240
So you can mitigate it before it's&nbsp;
ever isolated within a human host.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:08.510
I want to help build a deeper understanding of&nbsp;
well-conserved biological features - so that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:15.586
can make longer timescale predictions, so you&nbsp;
can better respond to biological rare events.

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:21.503
[Music]

00:02:21.503 --> 00:02:22.380
[Music fades to silence]

